WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.709
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday- what is this
weird, non-airport background?

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:07.880
Anyway, yesterday was the five year anniversary
of the signing of the Affordable Care Act,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.200
widely known as Obamacare, which made me wonder
if it's, ya'know, like, uh... worked.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.200
So here's the summary of what I found.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.500
In the United States in 2009 more of my tax
dollars went to healthcare than would if I

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.730
lived in Japan, or the United Kingdom, or
Australia, and in exchange for all of that

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.070
money we did not get the universal health
care that people in all of those countries

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.070
enjoy.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.020
Five years later, in 2014, we spent about
the same percentage of our GDP on health care,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:37.410
and we still don't have universal health care
but we do have less un-universal health care.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.410
By the way, I'm going to oversimplify here
a lot.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.940
For better information, you should check out
"Healthcare Triage".

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.550
So here's how it worked in the halcyon, pre-Obamacare
days of 2009: the government provided insurance

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.840
to elderly people, through Medicare, and also
to some poor people, through Medicaid.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.790
Many other people got insurance through their
jobs or paid for it themselves, but this still

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:59.730
left a lot of people out: like there were
44 million people without insurance.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.350
This was obviously a problem for those 44
million people, but it was also a problem

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:09.080
for the rest of us because we did have a kind
of universal healthcare in the United States:

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.830
like when uninsured people got sick and went
to the hospital, they did receive care and,

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.290
if later, they couldn't pay for that care,
those costs would be absorbed, either by the

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:21.530
rest of us paying higher prices for healthcare,
or else by taxes, because the government had

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.760
to subsidize public hospitals.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.380
But it wasn't really Universal Healthcare,
because you can't, like, go into an emergency

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.729
department and get your week's supply of insulin
if you're diabetic.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:36.189
So in the US we didn't, and still don't, have
real universal Healthcare, we have, like,

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:38.280
if-you-get-shot,-we-will-try-to-patch-that-up-for-you
universal Healthcare.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.750
And this system was astonishingly inefficient.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.970
Like, we spent more than 17% of our total
Gross Domestic Product on healthcare.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.450
In countries like Canada and France and Australia
and the Netherlands, they spend less than

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.909
10% of their GDP on healthcare, and they get
similar, or in many cases better, health outcomes.

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:59.420
Also, back in 2009, insurers could deny you
access to healthcare insurance if you had

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.950
a "pre-existing condition": a phrase intentionally
so broad that it encompassed, like, everything.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.790
For instance, Henry Reich of "Minute Physics"
was once denied insurance because he had the

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.090
pre-existing condition of tinnitus, or ringing
in his ear.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.090
The only way to get around the pre-existing
condition clause was if you worked for a big

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.300
company, you could get insurance through them.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.190
And that, of course, incentivized people to
work for big companies when they might otherwise

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.770
be doing different kinds of work, like...I
don't know, making "Minute Physics" videos.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.910
So, the Affordable Care Act sought to make
the labor market less bananas; decrease health

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.310
related bankruptcies, the number one cause
of bankruptcy in the United States; and, ultimately,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.879
to insure those 44 million people without
insurance, while, at the same time, over time

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:41.960
decreasing the amount of money we spend on
healthcare.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.959
It was going to solve the access problem by
creating these healthcare exchanges where

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:49.790
private companies could compete for people's
business, but they couldn't discriminate based

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.970
on gender or pre-existing conditions.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.030
The law also would expand Medicaid, which
would get insurance to more poor people, and

00:02:55.030 --> 00:03:00.090
it required companies with more than 50 employees
to provide affordable insurance to their employees,

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.349
or else pay a gigantic penalty.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:05.990
This would all be paid for, more than paid
for, hopefully, by cost control measures.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.320
These range from getting tougher on Medicare
fraud, to incentivizing hospitals to keep

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.440
elderly patients healthy so they don't get
readmitted to the hospital.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.790
There's also a new tax on, like, tanning salons,
and an increase in the Medicare payroll tax

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.629
for income over $250,000 a year.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:22.390
But has it worked?

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.170
YES - insofar as things are better than before.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:30.970
And also...NO - insofar as we are nowhere
close to a path to sustainable healthcare

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:31.970
spending.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.870
So the ACA was phased in slowly, and the real
meat of it didn't get going until 2014.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.851
You'll recall the roll-out of the healthcare
exchanges in late 2013 was...a complete disaster.

00:03:40.851 --> 00:03:46.040
The website, healthcare.gov, was plagued with
glitches and really just...didn't work.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.470
Admittedly, buying private insurance in the
US before the Obamacare exchanges was arguably

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.420
even worse than healthcare.gov.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:53.420
But it was just terrible.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.290
I remember trying to sign up in those early
months and it was truly awful.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.840
What has not been widely reported is that,
now, it's really easy.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.599
In general, the exchanges now work very well
and more than 12 million Americans, including

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:08.720
me, have signed up for insurance through them.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.300
Insurance premiums are going up, on average,
more slowly than they did before the exchanges,

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:16.769
and more insurers are participating, which
means there will be more competition in the

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:17.769
market.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:20.799
Plus, the law is costing less than expected
because fewer employers than expected have

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:22.780
dropped fewer people than expected off their
insurance.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.909
And, it doesn't seem to be negatively affecting
the job market.

00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:28.520
It certainly led to more freedom in the labor
market.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.159
For instance, Sarah Green could leave her
insurance-providing museum job to start an

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:34.619
art show with PBS Digital.

00:04:34.619 --> 00:04:38.560
And certainly for people who can get insurance
now who couldn't before, either because of

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.349
pre-existing conditions or because they couldn't
afford without the government subsidies, the

00:04:42.349 --> 00:04:44.410
ACA is a really big deal.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.280
And none of the collapse of the healthcare
system or "death panel" predictions have come

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:49.280
to pass.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.099
Instead, uninsurance rates in the U.S. have
dropped dramatically, and early signs indicate

00:04:53.099 --> 00:04:56.710
that at least in some sectors the law is improving
health.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.599
By the way, sources for all this stuff in
the doobly-doo.

00:04:58.599 --> 00:05:03.349
On the other hand, many millions of people
still don't have insurance and under-insurance,

00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:06.449
where people technically have insurance but
still end up with medical bills they can't

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:08.400
afford is a huge problem.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.650
And while the growth of our healthcare costs
has slowed, we still spend over 17 percent

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.219
of our GDP on healthcare.

00:05:15.219 --> 00:05:19.149
Most other developed countries spend far less
than that and still have citizens who live

00:05:19.149 --> 00:05:21.469
longer and healthier lives than Americans.

00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:26.059
And before you say that's because Americans
eat or drink or smoke or whatever more than

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:29.210
British people: no, that's simply not true!

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.069
Have you ever met an actual British person?

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:34.190
I don't mean like Jack or Finn or Charlie,
I mean a real one.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.649
So yeah, if nothing changes, healthcare costs
are still going to crush us in the long run,

00:05:37.649 --> 00:05:41.529
because the thing about the ACA is that it's
not particularly radical.

00:05:41.529 --> 00:05:46.460
Like, the 12 million people who got insurance
through the exchanges, that's less than 4%

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.460
of Americans.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.219
For the vast majority of people, very little
has changed, and that means that the underlying

00:05:51.219 --> 00:05:53.169
problems haven't really changed.

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:56.819
We spend way too much on healthcare, and we
get too little in return.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:06:00.979
To take that seriously would require radical
change: government interventions like price

00:06:00.979 --> 00:06:01.979
controls, for instance.

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:06.099
Or a single-payer healthcare system, or a
truly free market system where if you show

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:10.150
up to the hospital with a gunshot wound and
you can't pay for treatment, you don't get

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:11.150
treatment.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.419
So Hank, the old system definitely wasn't
working, and the ACA is working, in so far

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:20.139
as its job is to get health insurance to people
who don't have it without disturbing the health

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:22.089
insurance ecosystem for most people.

00:06:22.089 --> 00:06:27.740
But the ACA didn't really replace an existing
system so much as it grew on top of it.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.330
Now the fact that so many insurers are bringing
more plans to the exchanges indicates that

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:36.000
probably the ACA will continue to work, unless
the Supreme Court eviscerates it in a few

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.000
months.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.860
But the ACA has not and will not solve the
long-term healthcare spending challenge that

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:41.860
we face.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.610
Neither, of course, would repealing the law,
if anything the the healthcare spending problem

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.619
was worse before Obamacare.

00:06:46.619 --> 00:06:51.360
Rather than the endless discussions of repeal
or defend we should be frank about the choices

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.360
before us.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.339
As healthcare economist and healthcare triage
host Aaron Carroll told me, trade-offs are

00:06:56.339 --> 00:06:57.339
messy.

00:06:57.339 --> 00:07:01.089
You can get reform more easily and more cleanly,
but not necessarily while preserving the private

00:07:01.089 --> 00:07:02.089
market.

00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:05.409
You can make healthcare cheaper, but not without
imposing even more regulation.

00:07:05.409 --> 00:07:09.159
You can insure more people and make it cost
less for everyone, but not without spending

00:07:09.159 --> 00:07:10.159
more tax dollars.

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:14.229
Hank, healthcare policy experts across the
political spectrum seem to acknowledge this

00:07:14.229 --> 00:07:15.679
complex reality.

00:07:15.679 --> 00:07:19.339
It's our job now to make our politicians acknowledge
it too.

00:07:19.339 --> 00:07:22.680
Again, for more information and lots of other
videos about healthcare, please check out

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.680
Healthcare Triage.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.089
Hank, thanks for reminding me last week about
the many pleasures of being in a crowd, I

00:07:27.089 --> 00:07:28.349
will see you on Friday.

